-- title:
--   For $t \geq 3$ the natural logarithm exceeds $1$: $\log t > 1$
-- statement:
--   Let $t \in \mathbb{R}$ with $t \geq 3$. Then
--
--   $$\log t > 1.$$
--
--   This holds because $\log$ is strictly increasing and $\log 3 > 1$ (equivalently $e < 3$).
--
--   It is a small numeric fact used pervasively in the zeta-bound estimates, where error terms and zero-free-region widths are expressed in powers of $\log t$ for $t \geq 3$: knowing $\log t > 1$ lets one absorb lower-order log powers into higher ones and keeps constants explicit.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/AlexKontorovich/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/blob/f55e85551ac10e96d98262a354cfcaac2825f2da/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/ZetaBounds.lean#L1249-L1250

